WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.820
What happens when something holds hemoglobin in the wrong shape, or makes one shape

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:08.820
dangerous?

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:15.290
Carbon monoxide binds the heme about 250 times more tightly than oxygen does.

4
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:20.330
Like oxygen, it favors R. So it shifts the mix toward R.

5
00:00:21.283 --> 00:00:25.043
Predict. One subunit carries carbon monoxide.

6
00:00:25.943 --> 00:00:28.683
Is the oxygen on the other three held more or less tightly?

7
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:41.163
More tightly. The molecule is held in R, so its oxygen is released far less readily.

8
00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:43.203
The curve shifts left.

9
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:49.073
So carbon monoxide takes sites away from oxygen, and it makes the oxygen still

10
00:00:49.073 --> 00:00:50.073
aboard stay put.

11
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.583
Carbon monoxide hemoglobin is bright red.

12
00:00:54.343 --> 00:00:57.923
But the classic cherry-red skin is rarely seen in living patients.

13
00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:02.403
A pulse oximeter cannot tell it from oxygen hemoglobin.

14
00:01:03.243 --> 00:01:05.623
It reads falsely normal while tissues suffocate.

15
00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:11.703
The fix is high-concentration oxygen, to compete the carbon monoxide back off.

16
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:15.103
Sickle-cell disease is a different failure.

17
00:01:15.963 --> 00:01:17.643
Here the T shape itself turns sticky.

18
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:20.537
One amino acid changes.

19
00:01:21.537 --> 00:01:24.577
Glutamate becomes valine at position 6 of the β chain.

20
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.190
That valine sits on the surface in every state.

21
00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.310
But in T, a neighbor's hydrophobic pocket opens, and the valine plugs in.

22
00:01:34.283 --> 00:01:35.983
That neighbor sticks to the next.

23
00:01:36.823 --> 00:01:41.383
The molecules polymerize into stiff fibers that deform the red cell into a crescent.

24
00:01:42.383 --> 00:01:44.263
So the trigger is losing oxygen.

25
00:01:45.263 --> 00:01:46.903
Exertion raises oxygen use.

26
00:01:48.003 --> 00:01:49.483
Cold slows blood through small vessels.

27
00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:53.247
Both leave more hemoglobin in T for longer.

28
00:01:54.287 --> 00:01:58.167
Dehydration concentrates sickle hemoglobin, so fibers form faster.

29
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:01.917
Carriers with one copy are usually healthy.

30
00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:05.977
They are partly protected against severe falciparum malaria.

31
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.840
One newer treatment uses gene editing to switch fetal hemoglobin back on.

32
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.710
Fetal hemoglobin does not join the sickle polymer.

33
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:20.290
It dilutes the sickle hemoglobin around it, so cells sickle far less.

34
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:24.253
Almost everything in this story follows one rule.

35
00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:27.733
Hemoglobin is a mix of a weak shape and a tight shape.

36
00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:31.467
Oxygen shifting that mix is how blood delivers.

37
00:02:32.547 --> 00:02:35.127
Locking the mix, or making T sticky, is how it fails.
